WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.350
The 2010s was a roller
coaster of a decade for film.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.470
We saw Disney dominating
at the box office,

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.840
but we also saw the rise of streaming

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.880
and the decline of movie-ticket sales.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.319
Despite the fact that so many
amazing films were produced,

00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:15.960
we did manage to narrow down our list.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.652
So without any further ado,

00:00:17.652 --> 00:00:20.101
here are our top 10 movies of the 2010s.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.783
No. 10: "Call Me by Your Name."

00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:26.223
Oliver: Call me by your name,

00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:28.006
and I'll call you by mine.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.400
Narrator: The mood of
"Call Me by Your Name"

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.080
mirrors the hot and
relaxing Italian summer days

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.860
that are depicted in the film.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.430
Set in 1983, "Call Me by
Your Name" tells the story

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.420
of the slow-burn romance
between two young lovers,

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.110
Elio and Oliver.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.180
Luca Guadagnino's stylized
direction is entrancing,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.240
as are the performances by Armie Hammer

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.730
and Timothée Chalamet.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.730
And the movie also
features Michael Stuhlbarg

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.817
delivering one of cinema's
all-time-great monologues.

00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:57.200
"Call Me by Your Name"
is a realistic depiction

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.410
of young love that is amplified

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.180
by several tender original songs

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.950
from Sufjan Stevens.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.882
All in all, any movie
that can forever change

00:01:04.882 --> 00:01:06.840
the way that society looks at peaches

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.464
is a film worth celebrating.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.060
No. 9: "Eighth Grade."

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.430
Kayla: Being yourself can be hard.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.990
And it's like, aren't
I always being myself?

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.390
Narrator: Seeing life itself play out

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.710
with such specific detail can be scary,

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.840
which is exactly what
"Eighth Grade" delivers.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.880
The film follows Kayla Day in
her last week of eighth grade,

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.150
dealing with the difficult moments

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.850
that this time of life brings.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.150
She looks forward to the
next big step, high school,

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.320
and, like everyone, she wants
to be cool and confident.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.430
The honesty of the film rests heavily

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.080
on the shoulders of Elsie Fisher,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.120
who plays Kayla.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.300
Her performance is painfully recognizable,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.020
from the stuttering to the
subtlest of eye twitches.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.300
This film is very
specifically of its time.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.770
Kayla deals with social media, technology,

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:49.440
and changing social attitudes.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.770
But Bo Burnham's script
somehow also captures

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.170
something both individual and universal.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.920
Even if you haven't lived
as a middle schooler

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.500
in the time of YouTube videos

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.330
or the pervasive use of the internet,

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.640
even if you haven't
experienced the joys and pains

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.840
of American girlhood firsthand,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.912
at the heart of the film is an experience

00:02:05.912 --> 00:02:07.580
that we can all relate to.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.750
One of anxiety, wanting to be liked,

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.100
and having absolutely no
idea what we're doing.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.350
No. 8: "Spider-Man:
Into the Spider-Verse."

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.870
Peter Parker: What makes you different

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.560
is what makes you Spider-Man.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.393
Narrator: When talking about

00:02:21.393 --> 00:02:22.640
"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse,"

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.380
we have to talk about the visuals

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.320
and the technical
achievement of pulling off

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.960
the wildly artistic vision
of the art directors.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.000
The animation is the bold combination

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.330
of an uber-saturated 2D comic book,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.170
with its grid-like color cells,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.260
and the more realistic 3D
computer-generated animation.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.630
It's visually stunning

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.200
and, quite simply, like
nothing we've ever seen before.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.727
But visuals can only carry a movie so far.

00:02:45.727 --> 00:02:48.780
"Into the Spider-Verse" also
fully delivers on story,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.820
which is a tough challenge for any film,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.200
but especially a film
released in this context.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.880
We've seen far too many
iterations of Spider-Man

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.150
in less than two decades,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.320
and frankly, the zeitgeist
is a bit fatigued

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.840
by the constant reboots.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.040
In addition to the humorous
and whip-smart script,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.020
one of the main reasons
this spider-film is so fresh

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.170
is because our spider-protagonist
isn't Peter Parker at all.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.140
He's Miles Morales, a young
Afro-Latino kid from Brooklyn,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.237
bitten by a different radioactive spider.

00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:17.156
"Spider-Verse" is the best
animated film of the decade

00:03:17.156 --> 00:03:19.580
because, despite all the
cards stacked against it,

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.040
it's somehow the most original.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.107
No. 7: "The Florida Project."

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.120
Moonee: The man who lives
in here gets arrested a lot.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.573
These are the rooms we're
not supposed to go in.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.450
But let's go anyway.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.990
Narrator: The brightly
colored cinematography

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:36.833
in "The Florida Project" contrasts with

00:03:36.833 --> 00:03:38.800
the heavy nature of the film's subject

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.840
to create a darkly beautiful
film about childhood.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.040
The film is about
Moonee, a 6-year-old girl

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.410
living with her struggling
mother in one of the motels

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.520
near Disney World.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.350
The fully realized characters
are the heart of this film

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.740
and are elevated by the
amazing performances

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.090
by Willem Dafoe and the rest of the cast,

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.510
which is especially impressive

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.470
considering that no one besides Dafoe

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.770
had ever acted before in a film.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.670
Like in his previous film, "Tangerine,"

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.700
Sean Baker acts as writer,
director, and editor,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.300
proving that he is a master of his craft.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.650
Additionally, he avoids
exploiting the subject matter

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.360
of poverty in his film
through an extremely honest

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.540
and unbiased depiction of his characters.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.140
There are no protagonists and
no antagonists, just people.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.490
Baker has figured out how to distill

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.150
these seemingly simple moments

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.040
into an extraordinary film.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.103
Bobby: I love you too.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.242
Narrator: No. 6: "The Social Network."

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.360
He's directed so many
masterpieces at this point

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.460
that it seems rather superfluous
to praise David Fincher,

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:39.980
but when you combine Fincher

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.670
with an Aaron Sorkin screenplay,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.448
the result is something
that should probably

00:04:43.448 --> 00:04:45.100
be acknowledged and revered,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.570
which is exactly what we get
with "The Social Network."

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.780
On the surface level, "The
Social Network" is about the rise

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.390
of Mark Zuckerberg and Facebook,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.120
but the film is much more interested

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.210
in the human aspect of the story.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.068
As Sorkin said, "The invention itself

00:04:57.068 --> 00:04:58.584
is as modern as it gets,

00:04:58.584 --> 00:05:00.860
but the story is as old as storytelling;

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.950
the themes of friendship,
loyalty, jealousy,

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:04.740
class, and power."

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.280
Fincher brings Sorkin's
neo-Shakespearean screenplay

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.220
to life, perfectly balancing
a dozen characters,

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.690
nonlinear storytelling, and
intertwining narrative arcs.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.070
Jesse Eisenberg delivers
his best performance ever,

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.000
and when you put all of
those elements together,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.620
you get an outstanding film

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.853
that will hopefully
outlive Facebook itself.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:25.150
No. 5: "Lady Bird."

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:26.620
Lady Bird: I wanna go to the East Coast.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.510
I wanna go where culture is like New York.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:29.640
Marion: How in the world
did I raise such a snob?

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.420
Lady Bird: Or at least
Connecticut or New Hampshire,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.334
where writers live in the woods.

00:05:32.334 --> 00:05:34.370
Marion: You wouldn't get
into those schools anyway.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.203
Lady Bird: Mom!

00:05:35.203 --> 00:05:36.740
Narrator: Greta Gerwig and Saoirse Ronan

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.437
are a match made in
indie Hollywood heaven.

00:05:39.437 --> 00:05:42.050
"Lady Bird" carries the
same wit and tone we've seen

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.810
in films Gerwig has starred in,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.350
but with her at the reins
as writer and director,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.790
we get a window into her soul,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.270
as she shows us the painful

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.150
and endlessly funny roller coaster

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.970
that is the life of a teenage
Catholic school rebel.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.830
This semiautobiographical
film is crammed full

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.890
of genuine moments,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.512
showcasing the good, bad, and ugly aspects

00:06:00.512 --> 00:06:02.670
of family, friendship, and growing up.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.520
Ronan once again proved that she is

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.710
one of the most talented actresses ever

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.740
and works perfectly with Laurie Metcalf

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.790
to nail their contentious

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.880
mother-daughter relationship dynamic.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.010
Gerwig shows that she's
talented beyond her years,

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:16.600
as "Lady Bird" shrewdly
explores family dynamics

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.810
and social status in an extremely honest

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:19.910
and entertaining film.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.220
No. 4: "Mad Max: Fury Road."

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.470
Immortan Joe: Where is she taking them?

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.330
Narrator: Who would've
thought that an artistic,

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.810
feminist movie from the
man who made "Happy Feet"

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.720
would not only be one
of the best action films

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.030
of the decade but one of
the best films of all time?

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.470
Well, "Mad Max: Fury Road"
is all of that and more.

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:44.797
Set in the not-so-distant
dystopian future,

00:06:44.797 --> 00:06:47.260
"Mad Max" has a fairly
straightforward plot.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:49.880
Max and Furiosa attempt
to outrun and escape

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.800
the warlord Immortan Joe
and his deadly caravan.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.500
Technically, the film is flawless.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.640
Its acting, directing, stunts,
editing, and choreography

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.160
are all top notch.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.170
The film's stripped-down plot allows room

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:02.817
for the action to really shine.

00:07:02.817 --> 00:07:04.700
"Mad Max" has more memorable moments

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.710
in one five-minute action sequence

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:09.800
than other action movies
have in their entire runtime.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.750
The film is wild, immersive,
and hardly ever slows

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.250
from its full-throttle action insanity.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.768
No. 3: "Boyhood."

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:28.410
One of the many things that
makes "Boyhood" exceptional

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.840
is that it was filmed
over a 12-year period,

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.730
a singular and Herculean
filmmaking achievement.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.290
But "Boyhood" probably
would've had a very good shot

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.520
of making our top-10 list

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.410
even if it was produced
like a regular film

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:41.897
due to Richard Linklater's
beautiful storytelling.

00:07:41.897 --> 00:07:44.860
"Boyhood" follows the
day-to-day life of a boy, Mason,

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:46.200
played by Ellar Coltrane,

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.190
who started filming at the age of 6

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.160
and finished at the age of 18.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.410
Linklater created a fulfilling narrative,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.360
slowly piecing it together
as they filmed every summer

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.860
from 2002 to 2013.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.300
Patricia Arquette deservedly
won the Academy Award

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.620
for best supporting actress,

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.483
and Ethan Hawke turned in one of his

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:05.920
routinely outstanding
performances as well.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.130
A totally unique approach
to creating a film,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.320
combined with Arquette,
Hawke, and Linklater

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:11.750
at the height of their powers,

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.792
created this stellar,
one-of-a-kind masterpiece.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.810
No. 2: "Moonlight."

00:08:21.338 --> 00:08:22.721
Kevin: Who is you, Chiron?

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.717
Narrator: From director Barry Jenkins,

00:08:25.717 --> 00:08:27.540
"Moonlight" follows Chiron,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.360
a boy living in the projects of Miami,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.330
as he grapples with his harsh home life

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:32.690
and his sexuality.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.730
The film's earnest script is complemented

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.930
by a dreamy pastel color palette,

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:38.950
an aching score from Nicholas Britell,

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.270
and an outstanding cast
from top to bottom,

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.360
including an Academy
Award-winning performance

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:44.990
from Mahershala Ali.

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.500
It was also the first Academy
Award best-picture winner

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.060
to feature an all-black cast

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.037
and the first one that had
a prominent LGBT theme.

00:08:52.037 --> 00:08:54.380
"Moonlight" is a beautiful
and deeply moving

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.300
depiction of masculinity.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.560
Every aspect of the film is perfect,

00:08:58.560 --> 00:08:59.570
and yet, somehow,

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:02.800
it still manages to be more
than the sum of its parts.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.250
A magnum opus for everyone involved.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:09.310
No. 1: "Get Out."

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.176
Dean: So, how long has this been going on,

00:09:11.176 --> 00:09:13.243
this...this thang?

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.460
Narrator: Writer and director Jordan Peele

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.830
announced himself as an
auteur with "Get Out,"

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.580
a mystery-thriller that
is a spot-on commentary

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.235
about race in America.

00:09:22.235 --> 00:09:24.530
"Get Out" begins with a
black photographer, Chris,

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:26.840
tentatively agreeing to go visit and meet

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.650
with his white girlfriend's
family for the first time.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.440
Her family seems nice on the surface,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.220
but Chris can't help but
notice that something

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.530
seems strange about her parents' house.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.340
Peele's script is tense, funny,

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.550
and rewards viewers for repeat viewings

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.610
with dozens of Easter eggs.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.450
But more than anything, it's
an incredibly sharp critique

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:48.334
of liberal racism and the
lie of a post-racial America.

00:09:48.334 --> 00:09:50.630
We think that this film
is the best of the decade

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.900
for being exceedingly clever,
thrilling, rewatchable,

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.033
and for its scathing and
poignant social commentary.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.830
So, what did you think of our list?

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:02.770
What's your favorite?

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.778
Let us know in the comments.

